# Surface Passivation and Energetic Modification Suppress Nonradiative Recombination in Perovskite Solar Cells

## abstract
Surface passivation via post-treatment is an important strategy for improving power conversion efficiency and operational stability of perovskite solar cells. However, so far the interaction mechanisms between passivating additive and perovskite are not well understood. Here, we report the atomic-scale interaction of surface passivating additive 2,2-difluoroethylammonium bromine (2FEABr) on the MAPbI3. It is found that the bulky 2FEA+ cations tend to distribute at film surface, while the Br- anions diffuse from surface into bulk. A combination of 19F, 207Pb, and 2H solid-state NMR further reveal the Br- anions' partial substitution for the I- sites, the restricted motion of partial MA+ cations, and the firmed perovskite lattices, which would improve charge transport and stability of the perovskite films. Optical spectroscopy and ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy demonstrate that the 2FEABr induced surface passivation and energetic modification suppress the nonradiative recombination loss. These findings enable the efficiency of the p-i-n structured PSC significantly increasing from 19.44 to 21.06%, accompanied by excellent stability. Our work further establishes more knowledge link between passivating additive and PSC performance.

## HIGHLIGHTS
•	 The partial substitution of $\mathrm{Br-}$ on I-sites, and the restricted motion of $\mathrm{\Delta\mathbf{MA}^{+}}$ cations in correlation with suppressed electron-phonon coupling promote charge transport.
•	 The perovskite parent lattice of 2FEABr-treated perovskites was firmed, and the difficulty degree for A-site $\mathrm{\Delta\mathbf{MA}^{+}}$ cations running out of the inorganic framework was thus enhanced.
•	 The efficiency was enhanced from $19.44\%$ to $21.06\%$ , accompanied with excellent stability.
ABSTRACT  Surface passivation via post-treatment is an important strategy for improving power conversion efficiency and operational stability of perovskite solar cells. However, so far the interaction mechanisms between passivating additive and perovskite are not well understood. Here, we report the atomic-scale interaction of surface passivating additive 2,2-difluoroethylammonium bromine (2FEABr) on the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ . It is found that the bulky $2\mathrm{FEA}^{+}$ cations tend to distribute at film surface, while the $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ anions diffuse from surface into bulk. A combination of ${}^{19}\mathrm{F}.$ , $^{207}\mathrm{{Pb}}$ , and $^2\mathrm{H}$ solid-state NMR further reveal the $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ anions’ partial substitution for the $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ sites, the restricted motion of partial $\mathrm{\Delta\mathbf{M}A^{+}}$ cations, and the firmed perovskite lattices, which would improve charge transport and stability of the perovskite films. Optical spectroscopy and ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy demonstrate that the 2FEABr induced surface passivation and energetic modification suppress the nonradiative recombination loss. These findings enable the efficiency of the $p$ -i-n structured PSC significantly increasing from 19.44 to $21.06\%$ , accompanied by excellent stability. Our work further establishes more knowledge link between passivating additive and PSC performance.
KEYWORDS  Passivation; Solid-state NMR; Charge transport; Perovskite solar cell
## 1  Introduction
Organic–inorganic hybrid perovskites are promising semiconductors suitable for next generation solar energy technology owing to the excellent photoelectronic merits of high light absorption coefficient [1, 2], long electron–hole diffusion length [3, 4], tunable bandgap [5–7], and small exciton binding energy [8, 9]. Attributed to the implements of material compositional engineering [10–12], solvent engineering [13–15] and device architecture design [16–18], the certified power conversion efficiencies (PCE) of perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have reached $25.7\%$ within a decade of efforts [19]. However, the devices are suffering from significant nonradiative recombination losses, severely limiting their thermodynamic efficiency [20–23].
Tremendous experimental and theoretical works have demonstrated that defect passivation on polycrystalline perovskite films is a promising strategy for both suppressing carrier recombination and stabilizing perovskite structure [24–31]. The dominated defects locate at grain boundaries and on surface of perovskite films, and the surface defect densities are approximately two orders much than that in the bulk [32]. Among the various passivating additives reported, it has been well-established that ammonium salts can effectively reduce the defects by bonding with the Pb-I antisites or cation vacancies, thereby increasing the device performance [25, 33, 34]. Surface post-treatment is a commonly used approach to introduce these passivators into perovskite semiconductors. For instance, Hagfeldt and co-workers recently shown that a mixed-ammonium salt formamidinium bromide (FABr) via reacting with the excess $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ resulted in a high-quality perovskite surface with less defects and an improved $23.5\%$ efficiency in $n{-}i{-}p$ structural PSCs [35]. Stranks and co-workers reported that the ammonium salt methylammonium tetrafluoroborate $\mathrm{(MABF_{4})}$ ) could achieve the small nonradiative recombination with a reduction in first-order recombination rate in PSCs [36]. However, the atomic-scale spatial distribution of ammonium salt passivator in perovskite film, and the effect of passivator on perovskite lattice are unclear, which are critical to better understand the working mechanisms and thus provide guidance for designing more effective passivating additives towards high-performance PSCs.
In this work, we report the atomic-scale interaction of fluorinated ammonium salt 2,2-difluoroethylammonium bromine (2FEABr) surface passivating additive on hybrid perovskite $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ for photovoltaics. It is found that the bulky $2\mathrm{FEA}^{+}$ cations tend to distribute at film surface, while the $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ anions diffuse from surface into bulk. A combination of ${}^{19}\mathrm{F}$ , $^{207}\mathrm{{Pb}}$ , and $^2\mathrm{H}$ solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) technologies are carefully employed to systematically explore the subtle interactions between 2FEABr and $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ . We demonstrate that the $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ anions of 2FEABr partially substitute for the $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ sites in perovskite lattices, and the motion of partial $\mathrm{MA}^{+}$ cations is restricted in correlation with suppressed electron–phonon coupling, which are beneficial for suppressing nonradiative recombination. These findings are confirmed by the power conversion efficiency of the $p$ -i-n structured PSC significantly increasing from 19.44 to $21.06\%$ . Moreover, the unencapsulated device exhibits excellent thermal stability with maintaining over $95\%$ of its initial efficiency at $340\mathrm{K}$ after $1000\mathrm{h}$ . The NMR signals confirm that the perovskite parent lattice is firmed after 2FEABr post-treatment and the degree of difficulty of the $\mathrm{A}^{-}$ site $\mathrm{MA}^{+}$ cations running out of the inorganic framework is thus enhanced, leading to a robust perovskite film. This work provides more insights into working mechanisms of passivating additive for highly efficient and stable PSCs.
## 2  Experimental Section
## 2.1  Materials
Methylammonium iodide (MAI) was purchased from Greatcell Solar Company. Lead iodide $(\mathrm{PbI}_{2})$ was obtained from TCI. Phenyl-C61-butyric acid methyl ester (PCBM), Poly(triarylamine) (PTAA), Bathocuproine (BCP) and 2,3,5,6-Tetrafluoro-7,7,8,8-tetracyanoquinodimethane (F4-TCNQ) were brought from Xi’an Polymer Light Technology Corp. The anhydrous solvents including $N$ , $N_{\mathbf{\ell}}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF), chlorobenzene (CB) and isopropanol (IPA) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. 2,2-difluoroethylammonium bromine (2FEABr) was synthesized following the procedure from a literature [37].
## 2.2  Device Fabrication
The patterned ITO substrates (NSG 10, Nippon sheet glass) were cleaned by sequentially sonication with detergent, DI water, acetone, ethanol and IPA for each $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ , respectively. The cleaned ITO substrates were dried in an oven at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and treated with UV-ozone for $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ . $2~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ PTAA with $25\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ F4TCNQ in CB was spun onto the ITO substrates at $5000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30\mathrm{~s~}$ , and then annealed at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ . $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ DMF solvent was spin-coated to wet the PTAA layer surface at $4000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10\mathrm{~s~}$ . The perovskite precursor was prepared by mixing $497.8\mathrm{~mg~PbI}_{2}$ and $159\mathrm{\mg\MAI}$ in a mixed solvent of $750~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ DMF and $85~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ DMSO. The perovskite precursor solution was spin-coated on PTAA layer at $4000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30\mathrm{s}$ and $150~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ CB was dropped on the center of the film after 8 s processing, and then the perovskite film was put onto a hotplate at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ . For the post-treatment process, the synthesized 2FEABr was dissolved in IPA with the different concentrations $(1,2,3,5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ and spin-coated onto the perovskite surface at $6000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30~\mathrm{s}$ , followed by annealing at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ . $20~\mathrm{{mg}~\mathrm{{mL^{-1}}}}$ PCBM in CB was spin-coated onto the perovskite film at $1800~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $40\mathrm{~s~}$ , and $0.5~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ BCP in IPA was spin coated at $6000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30~\mathrm{s}$ to form an interfacial layer. Finally, $100{\mathrm{nm}}\operatorname{Ag}$ electrode were thermally evaporated in a vacuum chamber at the pressure of $2\times10^{-4}$ Pa with a shadow mask (aperture area: $0.05\mathrm{cm}^{2}.$ ).
an energy resolution of $50\mathrm{meV}.$ . All spectra were calibrated by referring to Fermi level and Au $\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{f}}_{7/2}$ position of the ${\mathrm{Ar}}^{+}$ ion sputter cleaned Au foil.
## 2.4  Solid‑State NMR Spectroscopy
$^2\mathrm{H}$ and $^{207}\mathrm{{Pb}}$ static nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) experiments were performed in a Bruker $300~\mathrm{MHz}$ NMR spectrometer operating at $46.07~\mathrm{MHz}$ for $^2\mathrm{H}$ and $62.73~\mathrm{MHz}$ for $^{207}\mathrm{{Pb}}$ . A Bruker two-channel static PE probe with a home-made $2.5~\mathrm{mm}$ coil was used to record the spectra. The $^2\mathrm{H}$ spectra were acquired using the solid echo sequence. The $^2\mathrm{H}$ pulse width was $2.5~{\upmu\mathrm{s}}$ at an RF field strength of $100~\mathrm{kHz}$ . $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ and $^{19}\mathrm{F}$ magic angle spinning (MAS) NMR spectra were recorded by a Brucker $500~\mathrm{MHz}$ NMR spectrometer at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at $10~\mathrm{kHz}$ . $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ chemical shifts were referenced to adamantane $(38.5\mathrm{ppm})$ . 19F chemical shifts were referenced to pure ­CFCl (0 ppm) using NaF $(-224~\mathrm{ppm})$ as a secondary reference. The powders were scrapped from glass substrates and packed in a MAS rotor in a $\Nu_{2}$ -filled glovebox before being transferred into the NMR probe.
## 2.3  Film Characterization
X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded using a Rigaku Smart Lab diffractometer with $\mathrm{Cu-K}\alpha$ radiation as the X-ray source. Atomic force microscopy (AFM) images were obtained from an atomic force microscope (Shimadzu, SPM-9700) in tapping mode. UV–vis absorption spectra were measured by a UV–vis spectrometer (Shimadzu 3600). Steady-state photoluminescence (PL) spectra were obtained using a fluorescence spectrometer (Edinburgh, FLS-980). Time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) was performed by a time-correlated single-photon counting (TCSPC) system. A picosecond laser with a wavelength of $485\ \mathrm{nm}$ (C10196, Hamamatsu) was employed to excite the films. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) measurements were conducted in an ultrahigh vacuum surface analysis system equipped with SCIENTA R3000 spectrometer with a base pressure of $10^{-10}$ mbar. XPS was measured using the monochromatic Al Ka $1486.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ as excitation source, and UPS employed the He $\mathrm{~I~}21.22\mathrm{~eV}$ as the excitation source with
## 2.5  Device Characterization
The $J{-}V$ curves of perovskite solar cells were characterized using a digital source meter (Keithley 2400) under Enlitech Solar Simulator (AM 1.5G, $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ) that calibrated by a standard silicon solar cell from NREL. The EQE spectra were obtained using an Enlitech QE-R system under AC mode. The Mott-Schottky curves were recorded at $1\mathrm{kHz}$ from an electrochemical workstation (CHI 650E). The electronic impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were performed at a bias of $0.8\mathrm{V}$ in the dark by an impedance spectroscope (PGSTAT302N, Autolab) with the frequency ranging from $1\mathrm{MHz}$ to $1\:\mathrm{Hz}$ .
Space charge limited current (SCLC) measurement was applied to determine the electron trap density and mobility using the electron-only device with a structure of ITO/ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}/2$ 2FEABr/PCBM/BCP/Ag. The trap state density $(N_{t})$ was determined by the onset of the trap filling limit volt age $(V_{\mathrm{TFL}})$ according to the equation of $\begin{array}{r}{V_{\mathrm{TFL}}=\frac{e N_{t}L^{2}}{2\varepsilon\varepsilon_{0}}}\end{array}$ , where $\scriptstyle{\varepsilon_{O}}$ is the permittivity of free space, $\varepsilon_{r}$ is the relative permittivity of perovskite, and $L$ is the thickness of perovskite layer. The electron mobility $(\mu_{e})$ is calculated by fitting the SCLC with the Motto-Gurney law of $\begin{array}{r}{J_{d}={\frac{9}{8}}\varepsilon\varepsilon_{0}\mu{\frac{V^{2}}{L^{3}}}}\end{array}$ , where $J$ is the current density, and $V$ is the base voltage.
## 3  Results and Discussion
2FEABr solution at $2~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ is spin-coated on $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite surface and annealed at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ . The surface post-treatment process and the 2FEABr molecular structure are illustrated in Fig. 1a. The distribution of $2\mathrm{FEA}^{+}$ cations and $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ anions in the perovskite film is intuitively confirmed by XPS depth profiling analysis of $\mathrm{~F~}1s$ and Br $3d$ core level spectra (Fig. 1b–c). The F 1s signal disappears after $3\mathrm{nm}$ etched from the film surface, while the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Br}}3d$ is clearly observed. It is thus concluded that the bulky $2\mathrm{FEA}^{+}$ cations tend to distribute at surface, while the $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ anions could diffuse from surface into the bulk. The incorporation of $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ in perovskites has been demonstrated to enhance the carrier lifetime, which could improve the device optoelectronic performance [38, 39]. In addition, there is no clear change in perovskite XRD patterns before and after 2FEABr post-treatment (Fig. S1). To obtain more details of the crystal structure, we also performed the gracing incidence X-ray diffraction (GIXRD) measurement (Fig. S2). No new diffraction peak is observed when the incident angle increased from 0.3 to $5^{\circ}$ , indicating no low dimensional phase after 2FEABr treatment.
The $^{19}\mathrm{F}$ magic angle spinning (MAS) NMR spectroscopy is employed to study the environment of $2\mathrm{FEA}^{+}$ cations in pure 2FEABr and 2FEABr treated $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ (Fig. 1d). For the pure 2FEABr, the $^{19}\mathrm{F}$ NMR signal shows a pronounced asymmetric peak centered at $-117.5~\mathrm{ppm}$ with full width at half maximum (FWHM) of only $6\mathrm{ppm}$ .
The spike like signals marked by the asterisks are the sideband manifolds. For 2FEABr treated $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ , the $^{19}\mathrm{F}$ NMR appears as a wide peak with FWHM of $119\ \mathrm{ppm}$ . Intriguingly, the sideband manifolds are not observed here, meaning that the local environments of $^{19}\mathrm{F}$ in perovskite are quite different from those in the pure 2FEABr [40]. This pronounced change is attributed to the enhancement of dipolar coupling, indicating that the $2\mathrm{FEA^{+}}$ cations bond with $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ and interact with surface Pb-I antisite defects through electrostatic interaction [41–43]. Figures 1e and S4 present the $^{207}\mathrm{{Pb}}$ NMR spectra of the pristine and 2FEABr treated perovskites. The $^{207}\mathrm{{Pb}}$ spectra of the pristine $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ shows a line shape of a symmetrical broad peak centered at 1409 ppm, and the 207Pb NMR signal of the 2FEABr treated $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ shows a clear asymmetric peak, consisting of two components centered at 1401 and $1219\mathrm{ppm}$ , respectively. The former corresponds to $\mathrm{[PbI_{6}]^{4-}}$ structure of the pristine $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ , and the latter is probably attributed to $[\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{6-x}\mathrm{Br}_{x}]^{4=}$ species, where the $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ anions have partially substituted for the $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ sites [44]. In principle, the Br substitution will cause the formation of the disordered structures in the lattice. However, no clear evidence of such disordered structures was observed in the XRD results (Fig. S1), which means that the $^{207}\mathrm{{Pb}}$ NMR is a more sensitive technique to probe the disordered structures in the crystal lattice of perovskites.
Attributing to the subtle interaction between the embedded organic cations and inorganic frameworks in perovskite lattice, the molecular motion of the embedded organic cation is often very sensitive to the lattice change. The lattice changes thus can be monitored by observing the molecular motion change of the embedded cations [45, 46]. Figure 1f and S5 show the corresponding $^2\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra of the pristine and 2FEABr treated $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ . The $^2\mathrm{HNMR}$ spectra of the pristine $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ has a typical Pake pattern with a splitting of $4\mathrm{kHz}$ between the two horns. A new complicated line shape consisting of a narrow single peak and a broad characteristic component appears in 2FEABr treated $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ , whereas the original Pake line shape completely vanishes. The motional geometry of the $\mathrm{MA}^{+}$ cations is further analyzed by the pattern simulation based on the different motion models (Figs. S6 and S7). The Pake line shape in the $\mathrm{\Omega^{2}H N M R}$ spectra of the pristine $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ indicates that the $\mathrm{MA}^{+}$ cations undergo a double rotation, that is, a rotation of the ${\mathrm{ND}}_{3}$ group about the $\mathrm{C-N}$ axis $(R_{1}$ axis) and a rotation of $\mathrm{C-N}$ axis about $R_{2}$ axis (Fig. 1g). The complicated line shape of 2FEABr treated $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ can be decomposed into a hump component together with a broad component (detailed information in Fig. S7). The broad component (red) with a splitting of about $40\mathrm{kHz}$ is attributed to completely restricted rotation of $\mathrm{C-N}$ axis about $R_{2}$ axis in partial $\mathrm{MA}^{+}$ cations (the left model in Fig. 1h). Meanwhile, the emerged hump component (blue) with FWHM of $5\mathrm{\kHz}$ points to the inconsistency of rotation angle of $\mathrm{\Delta\mathbf{MA}^{+}}$ cations, indicating diverse isotropic motion components of $\mathrm{MA}^{+}$ cations in lattice (the right model in Fig. 1h). The restricted motion of the partial $\mathrm{MA}^{+}$ cations correlates with suppressed electron–phonon coupling, which is expected to suppress nonradiative recombination and enhance charge mobility [47, 48].
The root mean square (RMS) roughness of the 2FEABr treated perovskite films measured via AFM decreases from 20.46 to $17.74\mathrm{nm}$ , in favor of the perovskite contacting with the top charge transport layer (Fig. S8). The UV–vis absorption spectra display an obvious blue shift due to $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ doped into the perovskite lattice (Fig. S9). We then perform PL spectra to investigate the charge dynamics of the films. The improved PL intensity provides the solid evidence that 2FEABr helps to suppress nonradiative recombination by $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ substitution, passivate defect and restrict $\mathrm{\Delta\mathbf{MA}^{+}}$ motion (Fig. 2a). From the fitted time-resolved PL decays (Fig. 2b and Table S2), the 2FEABr treated perovskite films exhibit longer carrier lifetime $_{(\tau_{1}=32.3}$ ns, $\tau_{2}=145.6$ ns) than the pristine films ${\bf\chi}_{,\tau_{1}=20.5}$ ns, $\tau_{2}=101.8~\mathrm{ns}$ ), further confirming that the 2FEABr suppresses the charge recombination in the perovskite films (Fig. 2b). We evaluate the charge trap density and mobility of the 2FEABr treated perovskite films using the space-charge-limited current (SCLC) method. As determined from the dark J-V curves of electron-only device with a structure of $\mathrm{ITO/SnO_{2}/M A P b I_{3}/2F E A B r/P C B M/}$ $\mathbf{BCP/Ag}$ , the calculated electron trap densities dimmish from $1.18\times10^{16}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ for the pristine to $5.39\times10^{15}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ for the 2FEABr treated perovskite (Fig. 2c). The electron mobility also increases from $4.33\times10^{-2}$ to $6.45\times10^{-1}\ \mathrm{cm^{2}\ V^{-1}\ s^{-1}}$ , nearly one order of magnitude, boosting the charge transport. Moreover, we characterize surface electronic structures of the perovskite films by ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) (Fig. 2d). The secondary electron cutoff region determines the work function (WF), where the WF of the perovskite film decreases from 4.74 to $4.55\mathrm{eV}$ after 2FEABr post-treatment, which could further improve the electron extraction efficiency at perovskite/the top electron transport layer interface. In addition, the Fermi level $(E_{F})$ position with respect to the valence band maximum (VBM) shifts away from 0.69 to $1.19\mathrm{eV},$ , indicating the formation of more $n$ -type perovskite surface that matches with the top electron transport layer [49, 50]. Figure S10 presents the corresponding energy level diagram.
To confirm the effect of 2FEABr surface treatment on device performance, we fabricate the $p{-}i{-}n$ planar heterojunction PSC with an architecture of ITO/PTAA:F4TCNQ/ perovskite/2FEABr/PCBM/BCP/Ag as illustrated in Fig.  S12. The dependence of the device efficiency on
2FEABr concentration is carefully evaluated (Fig. S13 and Table S3), and the optimal concentration that produces the champion device is $2~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ . Figure 3a displays the current density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves of the devices under a simulated AM $1.5\mathrm{G}$ light illumination at $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The pristine device has a typical PCE of $19.44\%$ with an open circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ of $1.090\mathrm{V},$ , a short current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ of $22.38\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , and a fill factor (FF) of 0.797. The champion device with 2FEABr yields a significantly improved PCE of $21.06\%$ with a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.166\mathrm{V}$ , a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $22.39\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , and an FF of 0.807 (Table 1). Clearly, the enhanced device efficiency originates from the increased $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and FF.
Figure 3b shows the external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra and the integrated photocurrent densities. The absorption edge of the 2FEABr treated device is blue shifted about $20\mathrm{nm}$ , in consistent with the UV–vis absorption spectra shift of the perovskite film observed in Fig. S9, which leads to the nearly same photocurrents of the devices before and after 2FEABr treatment. The integrated current densities obtained from EQE are $21.28~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}$ for the pristine device and $21.20\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for the 2FEABr treated device, respectively, which are comparable with $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ in the $J{-}V$ curves. The statistical charts of the photovoltaic parameters PCE, $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and FF, demonstrate the good reproducibility of the device performance (Figs. 3c and S14). As shown in Fig. 3d, the 2FEABr treated device exhibits a stabilized power output of $20.63\%$ and a steady-state current density of $21.05~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at the maximum power point (MPP) under continuous illumination for $300\mathrm{s}$ .
To better understand the enhanced $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ , we carry out capacitance–voltage $(C-V)$ curves to directly compare the built-in potential $(V_{\mathrm{bi}})$ of the devices by the Mott-Schottky equation of 12 $\begin{array}{r}{\frac{1}{C^{2}}=\frac{2\left(V_{\mathrm{bi}}^{\mathrm{-}}-V\right)}{A^{2}e\varepsilon_{0}\varepsilon N_{A}}}\end{array}$ , where $A$ is device area, $\varepsilon$ is relative =   
permittivity, $\ensuremath{\varepsilon}_{0}$ is vacuum permittivity, and $N_{A}$ is carrier concentration. The $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ of the devices largely increases from 0.85 to $0.96{\mathrm{~V~}}$ after 2FEABr treatment (Fig. 3e), in agreement with the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ enhancement. We ascribe the increased flat band potential to the reduced work function and defect passivation of the perovskite surface. As shown in Fig. 3f, the slope of the dependence of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ on light intensity $(P)$ is conducted to explore the degree of the trap-assisted recombination via the equation of Voc = qlnk(TP) , where k is Boltzmann constant, and $T$ is absolute temperature. Compared to the slope of $1.66~\mathrm{kT}/q$ for the pristine device, the slope deceases to $1.32\mathrm{kT}/q$ for the 2FEABr treated device, demonstrating that the trap-assisted recombination is effectively suppressed. These results accord with the increased recombination
resistance $(R_{\mathrm{rec}})$ fitted in the electrical impedance spectroscopy (Fig. S15 and Table S4).
The device stability before and after 2FEABr treatment is comparatively explored. Under ambient air in $40\pm10\%$ relative humidity (RH) at room temperature, the unencapsulated device retains over $90\%$ of its initial efficiency after $1000\mathrm{h}$ storage, whereas the pristine device losses $60\%$ of its initial value (Fig. 4a), mainly attributed to the 2FEABr coated on the perovskite film surface acting like moisture protection layer and the more hydrophobicity of the 2FEABr treated perovskite film confirmed by the increased water contact angel (Fig. S16). The device thermal stability is also significantly enhanced at $340\mathrm{~K~}$ in a nitrogen-filled glovebox, where the unencapsulated device maintains over $95\%$ of its initial efficiency after $1000\mathrm{h}$ storage, and the pristine device losses nearly $40\%$ of its initial value (Fig. 4b). In addition, we measure the XRD patterns of the films before and after $500\mathrm{{h}}$ calcination at $340~\mathrm{K}$ (Fig. S17). It is clear that the pristine $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ significantly degrades to $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , while the 2FEABr treated film has negligible degradation under the same conduction. To gain more insight on the much-enhanced thermal stability of the device, the effect of 2FEABr on the perovskite lattice is explored via a combination of XRD and $^2\mathrm{H}$ NMR measurements. The XRD (211)
characteristic peaks at $23.5^{\circ}$ disappear when being heating at $340\mathrm{K}$ for both the pristine and 2FEABr treated perovskite films (Fig. S18), indicating a phase transition from tetragonal to cubic phase (Fig. S19) [51]. However, the XRD (100) peak of the 2FEABr treated $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ has the smaller shift of $0.04^{\circ}$ than $0.08^{\circ}$ of the pristine film after heating at $340\mathrm{~K~}$ (Fig. 4c–d), meaning less lattice expansion. Figure 4e shows the $^2\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra of the pristine $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ at 298 and $340\mathrm{K}$ , respectively. For the pristine $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ , the line shape completely changes from Pake doublets (298 K) to a narrow single peak $(340\mathrm{K})$ , implying the reorientation dynamics of $\mathrm{\Delta\mathbf{M}A^{+}}$ cations transformation from anisotropic into isotropic motion. For comparison in Fig. 4f, in the 2FEABr treated perovskites the original line shape of the $^2\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra does not completely turn into a narrow single peak, which indicates that the dynamics of $\mathrm{\Delta\mathbf{MA}^{+}}$ is still anisotropic and the lattice expansion is slight at the elevated temperature of $340\mathrm{K}$ . We conclude that the apparent NMR signal change results from the change of the cation dynamics due to the phase transition in $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ , and the perovskite parent lattice is firmed after 2FEABr post-treatment and thus the degree of difficulty of the A-site $\mathrm{\Delta\mathbf{M}A^{+}}$ cations running out of the inorganic framework is enhanced, leading to a robust perovskite film [52, 53].
## 4  Conclusion
In summary, we have investigated the subtle interactions of ammonium salt passivating additive 2FEABr and hybrid perovskite $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ for photovoltaics. It is found that the bulky $2\mathrm{FEA}^{+}$ cations tend to distribute at surface, while the $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ anions diffuse from surface into the bulk. SS-NMR further demonstrates that the atomic-scale information on the $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ anions’ partial substitution for the $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ sites, the restricted motion of partial $\mathrm{\Delta\mathbf{M}A^{+}}$ cations, and the firmed perovskite lattices in the 2FEABr treated perovskites, which boost the charge transport and stability of the perovskite film. Meanwhile, the 2FEABr induced surface passivation and energetic modification suppress the nonradiative recombination loss. These findings are confirmed by the efficiency of the $p{-}i{-}n$ structured device significantly increasing from 19.44 to $21.06\%$ , accompanied by excellent stability. Our work provides more insights into the working mechanisms between passivating additive and perovskite towards highly efficient and stable PSCs.
